//? Title: Exact Cover: Actual Choices, Dancing Links and Complete Restoration
//? Subtitle: Checked iterative Algorithm X, native domino encodings and bounded search witnesses
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Choose rows of a binary incidence matrix so that every required column
appears exactly once. We start with three columns and four rows: two
candidate choices fail, then a third succeeds. The same native C++
kernel captures actual choices, residual column sizes and initial/final
dancing-links meshes. It retains the original seven-column example and
4×6 domino tiling, while exposing finite input contracts, restoration
work and optional history costs. The solver finds the first cover;
it neither enumerates all covers nor makes an exponential search cheap
in every case.
)

\toc

> Three columns: two failed choices, then a cover

Use rows 0={0,1}, 1={0,2}, 2={1,2}, 3={0,1,2}. Every column has
three candidates initially, so the minimum-size tie picks column zero.
Choosing row zero leaves column two with no compatible row; choosing
row one leaves column one with none. After undoing each failed branch,
row three covers all columns and succeeds. Pair rows alone cannot cover
an odd number of required columns without an overlap or omission.

The diagram reads actual residual column-size snapshots. A gray box
means the column is already covered, a red zero is an active impossible
column, and a green number is the actual remaining candidate count.
The trace continues with restoration after the first solution is saved.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/exact_cover_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const auto t=dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(3,{{0,1},{0,2},{1,2},{0,1,2}});const auto& w=t.outcome.work;
  std::print("Tiny: solved {}; selected row {}; attempts {}; dead ends {}; maximum depth {}; events {}; entries {}; actual mesh restored {}.\n",t.outcome.result.solved,t.outcome.result.rows.at(0),w.row_attempts,w.dead_ends,w.maximum_depth,t.steps.size(),t.captured_entries,t.initial_nodes==t.final_nodes);
  std::print("Restoration: covers {}; uncovers {}; row restorations {}; header writes {}; data writes {}; size updates {}.\n\n",w.covers,w.uncovers,w.row_restorations,w.header_link_writes,w.data_link_writes,w.column_size_updates);
  const std::array<std::string_view,6> names{"choose","select","unselect","dead end","solution","finished"};
  std::print("| actual event | kind | depth | column | row | residual sizes | partial rows |\n| ---: | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.steps.size();++i){const auto& e=t.steps[i];std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |",i,names.at(static_cast<std::size_t>(e.kind)),e.depth,e.column,e.row);for(int value:e.column_sizes)std::print(" {}",value);std::print(" | ");for(int row:e.partial_rows)std::print("{} ",row);std::print("|\n");}
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle cell;cell.width=1.7;cell.height=1.1;cell.font_size=21;int line=0;
  for(const auto& e:t.steps)if((e.kind==dm::algorithms::ExactCoverEvent::choose_column&&e.depth==0)||e.kind==dm::algorithms::ExactCoverEvent::select_row){const double y=-2.*line++;
    d.text({-3,y},e.kind==dm::algorithms::ExactCoverEvent::choose_column?"initial":std::format("try row {}",e.row),23);
    for(std::size_t c=0;c<e.column_sizes.size();++c){const int n=e.column_sizes[c];cell.fill=n<0?dm::graphics::RGB{226,232,240}:n==0?dm::graphics::RGB{254,202,202}:dm::graphics::RGB{187,247,208};d.node(std::format("{}:{}",line,c),{2.2*c,y},std::format("column {}\n{}",c,n<0?"covered":std::to_string(n)),cell);}}
  d.text({1,2},"Actual residual columns at each attempted row",26).text({1,-8},"Red: impossible active column; gray: covered",22).save_svg("build/exact_cover_tiny.svg");
}
