> Algorithm X and why reversible links preserve alternatives

Let A be the binary incidence matrix and x a binary row-selection
vector. Exact cover asks for

\[
A^T x=\mathbf 1,\qquad x\in\{0,1\}^{R}.
\]

Every current partial selection consists of disjoint rows. If required
columns remain, choose one c and try each remaining row containing c.
Any complete cover extending the partial selection must use exactly
one such row, so these branches exhaust the possibilities. Removing
all columns of a selected row, and all conflicting rows, preserves
exactly its compatible extensions. An empty required column makes
that branch impossible; no remaining required columns means a cover
has been found. Induction over these branches gives soundness and
completeness of depth-first search, provided each failed branch restores
the previous state before the next alternative.

Dancing links stores each incidence in a circular row list and a
circular column list, with a header for each required column. For a
node x, unlinking it vertically performs

\[
U[D[x]]=U[x],\qquad D[U[x]]=D[x].
\]

Its own saved U[x],D[x] remain available. Restoring in inverse traversal
order performs U[D[x]]=x and D[U[x]]=x. Column headers are similarly
removed/restored in their horizontal list. A complete column cover
performs many such local operations and updates column sizes; it is
not just four assignments for an entire arbitrary row or column.

The canonical implementation uses integer node indices and an explicit
search stack. It preserves the minimum-size-column heuristic, earliest
column ties and original row order, while avoiding recursive call-stack
growth. It fully unwinds after success as well as failure, retaining
the first solution separately. The solution event therefore precedes
cleanup; an unselect event can mean successful cleanup rather than a
failed branch. Actual node snapshots verify complete restoration,
without rerunning another explanatory solver.

> Retain the original seven-column fixture

The original six-row example returns the row sequence 4,1,3. That is
a search order, not sorted row order. Those sets cover every column
once. We also run the literal matrix in Knuth's paper. The original
report's fixture changes row order and omits one incidence from one
of the paper's rows, so we identify the two fixtures separately rather
than calling them the same matrix. Both are explicitly defined below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/exact_cover_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const std::vector<std::vector<int>> rows{{0,3,6},{0,3},{3,4,6},{2,4,5},{1,6},{2,5}};const auto t=dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(7,rows);
  const auto paper=dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(7,{{2,4,5},{0,3,6},{1,2,5},{0,3},{1,6},{3,4,6}});
  std::print("Original seven-column selection:");for(int row:t.outcome.result.rows)std::print(" {}",row);std::print(". Literal paper-matrix selection:");for(int row:paper.outcome.result.rows)std::print(" {}",row);std::print(".\n");
  std::print("Original nodes {}; events {}; captured entries {}; scans {}; restored mesh {}.\n\n",t.initial_nodes.size(),t.steps.size(),t.captured_entries,t.outcome.work.column_scans,t.initial_nodes==t.final_nodes);
  std::print("| original row | selected | column memberships |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(64,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle cell;cell.width=.8;cell.height=.65;cell.font_size=19;cell.padding=5;
  for(std::size_t r=0;r<rows.size();++r){const bool selected=std::find(t.outcome.result.rows.begin(),t.outcome.result.rows.end(),int(r))!=t.outcome.result.rows.end();std::print("| {} | {} |",r,selected);for(int c:rows[r])std::print(" {}",c);std::print(" |\n");d.text({-1.3,-1.05*r},std::format("row {}",r),21);
    for(int c=0;c<7;++c){const bool present=std::find(rows[r].begin(),rows[r].end(),c)!=rows[r].end();cell.fill=present?(selected?dm::graphics::RGB{187,247,208}:dm::graphics::RGB{203,213,225}):dm::graphics::RGB{255,255,255};cell.border=selected?dm::graphics::RGB{22,163,74}:dm::graphics::RGB{148,163,184};d.node(std::format("{}:{}",r,c),{1.15*c,-1.05*r},present?"1":"0",cell);}}
  for(int c=0;c<7;++c)d.text({1.15*c,1},std::format("c{}",c),20);
  d.text({3,2.2},"Original fixture: selected rows 4, 1, 3",26).text({3,-6.7},"Green selected rows place exactly one 1 in each column",22).save_svg("build/exact_cover_original.svg");
}
