> Required-column contracts, complete restoration and capture limits

The API treats every column as required. Encodings with at-most-once
constraints, such as N-queens diagonals, require additional modelling
or a secondary-column variant. Within one row, each column ID must be
distinct and in range; unsorted entries are accepted without reordering.
Duplicate complete rows remain separate alternatives. Empty rows are
ignored, and an empty required universe succeeds with no selected rows.
Negative counts, invalid IDs and repeated incidences fail explicitly.
Root/header/incidence node counts and row IDs must fit the checked
integer and container geometry before allocation. Existing Sudoku
integration tests exercise the same required-column solver.

Work counts include all actual search and restoration operations, with
initialization, validation, copying and CPU time excluded. Ordinary
result/work calls keep the sparse runtime mesh, explicit stack and
owned result, without optional history or capture quotas. Search can
still require exponential work. The trace owns input rows, the actual
initial/final node records, and event snapshots. Equality of complete
initial/final arrays checks all seven integer fields of every node,
including links, column identities, row identities and sizes.

Let R be input row count, I incidence count and K=1+C+I the node count.
Known base B=R+I+2K counts row records, incidence entries and two full
node-record snapshots. Each complete seven-field node counts as one
record. Each event adds 1+C+depth: an event record, the full column-size
vector and the actual partial-row IDs. B is reserved before graph or
copy allocation; each event is reserved before its snapshot copies.
The default quota is one million; zero disables only that quota.
Ordinary result/work, runtime mesh/stack/validation storage, vector
headers, capacities and allocator bytes are excluded. This mixed
logical count is not peak RAM. A quota exception means the requested
history did not fit; it is not an unsatisfiability verdict, and no
partial trace is returned. Large unsatisfiable board tests therefore
use the ordinary solver, with bounded trace checks on smaller cases.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/exact_cover_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");const std::vector<std::vector<int>> rows{{0,1},{0,2},{1,2},{0,1,2}};const auto t=dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(3,rows,99);bool short_cap=false,base_cap=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(3,rows,98);}catch(const std::length_error&){short_cap=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(3,rows,38);}catch(const std::length_error&){base_cap=true;}
  std::size_t incidence=0;for(const auto& row:rows)incidence+=row.size();const auto input=rows.size()+incidence,meshes=2*t.initial_nodes.size();std::size_t events=0;for(const auto& e:t.steps)events+=1+t.num_columns+e.partial_rows.size();if(input+meshes+events!=t.captured_entries)throw std::runtime_error("capture certificate failed");
  std::print("Tiny quota: input {}; meshes {}; events {}; total {}; one-short rejected {}; base-short rejected {}; disabled-quota total {}.\n",input,meshes,events,t.captured_entries,short_cap,base_cap,dm::algorithms::exact_cover_trace(3,rows,0).captured_entries);
  int rejected=0;try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover(-1,{});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  for(const auto& bad:std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>>{{{-1}},{{1}},{{0,0}}})try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover(1,bad);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover(0,{{0}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  const auto duplicate=dm::algorithms::exact_cover(2,{{},{1,0},{1,0},{}});const auto empty=dm::algorithms::exact_cover(0,{{},{}});bool geometry=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::exact_cover_node_count(std::numeric_limits<int>::max(),0,0);}catch(const std::length_error&){geometry=true;}
  std::print("Domains: negative-count/negative-ID/high-ID/repeated-incidence/nonempty-zero-universe rejections {}/5; checked geometry rejected {}. Duplicate whole rows choose {}; empty universe solved {} with {} selected rows.\n",rejected,geometry,duplicate.rows.at(0),empty.solved,empty.rows.size());
  constexpr int n=4096;std::vector<std::vector<int>> deep(n);for(int i=0;i<n;++i)deep[i]={i};const auto r=dm::algorithms::exact_cover_work(n,deep);
  std::print("Deep ordinary search: solved {}; depth {}; chosen rows {}; header scans {}; covers {}; uncovers {}; optional history retained false.\n",r.result.solved,r.work.maximum_depth,r.result.rows.size(),r.work.column_scans,r.work.covers,r.work.uncovers);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(65,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.8;box.height=1.6;box.font_size=21;
  d.node("input",{0,0},std::format("Input {}\nrow + incidence entries",input),box).node("meshes",{5,0},std::format("Two meshes {}\nnode records",meshes),box).node("events",{10,0},std::format("Actual events {}\nrecords + vector entries",events),box).edge("input","meshes").edge("meshes","events")
   .text({5,2.1},std::format("Mixed logical entries: {} + {} + {} = {}",input,meshes,events,t.captured_entries),26).text({5,-1.9},"Output, runtime and allocator bytes excluded",22).save_svg("build/exact_cover_limits.svg");
}
