\caption(The exact 99-entry tiny trace succeeds, while both a one-short quota and insufficient known-base quota fail. The 4,096-level ordinary solve uses explicit frames and retains no optional history.)
\alttext(A native three-box diagram splits the actual tiny trace into thirteen input entries, twenty-six node records and sixty event/vector entries, totaling ninety-nine logical entries.)

The native tests compare all 5,058 binary matrices with up to three
columns and four rows, plus 500 seeded random matrices, against an
independent subset oracle. Every actual event is checked against an
independent set-based residual calculation; complete node lists are
checked for circular-link and membership invariants before/after search.
All 565 frozen valid legacy results retain the same selected row order.
The tests also cover 63 rectangular encodings, actual work/history
identities, deep explicit-frame search, ownership, exact quotas, invalid
incidence and geometry, and malformed graphics requests. The original
invalid-column probe caused a heap-buffer overflow; repeated IDs within
a row were previously accepted. Both now produce explicit input errors.

The inputs here are finite set systems and generated board encodings;
a real-world dataset would add no evidence about reversible links,
search completeness or the exact count identities. All reusable solving,
board generation and tiling validation/rendering live in the native core.

> Reference

D. E. Knuth (2000), Dancing Links, arXiv:cs/0011047,
https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0011047.
The required-column Algorithm X, minimum-size heuristic and reversible
index-link operations follow this paper. The explicit search frames,
complete success cleanup, checked domains, bounded live snapshots and
native board interfaces are datamunge implementation choices.
